Explain pytest.fixtures used in Playwright/
ANS: They are used in setup and teardown test conditions just like in a restaurant , how a table gets cleaned before eating and after eating.. This helps in pytest to actual focus on test function or test steps. 

When pytest.fixtures is combined with playwright python , fixtures allow you to inject pre-configured resources like fresh browser page, or a logged-in-session directly to the testcase. 

When we use pytest-playwright, it automatically provides built-in fixtures. 

These built in fixtures are passed as ARGUMENTS to test function. It is like providing a football stadium with pitch, chairs so that the match can start immediately. 

Some built in fixtures are page, browser, context. [CODE: BCP]

Browser (Grand parent)---> Context(parent)--> Page(grandchild)
browser: The Entire FIFA Organization / Infrastructure. It is heavy, expensive to spin up, and manages everything globally. launches the browser instance like chromium, chrome
context: An Isolated Locker Room. It is entirely separated from other locker rooms. What happens in Team A's locker room (cookies, storage, logins) cannot be seen by Team B.
page: A Player on the Pitch. This is the individual tab actually performing the actions.
When to use each fixture (What to tell the interviewer)
Use browser when: You need complete manual control over session creation. This is rare and mostly used for advanced multi-browser testing or modifying deep browser-level flags.
Use context when: You want to test features like opening a new tab (context.new_page()), checking if a pop-up window opens correctly, or granting specific geolocation permissions (e.g., simulating a fan opening the FIFA app while physically inside Mexico, Canada, or the USA).
Use page when: You are doing standard end-to-end testing (95% of test cases). It automatically creates a browser and a context behind the scenes for you, saving boilerplate code.


